WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
Boba Fett is one of the most iconic&nbsp;
characters in the Star Wars universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.080
He might be just one man, but he's&nbsp;
been played by over a dozen actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.040
since the character was first created.&nbsp;
Think you can you name them all?

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.640
Before the release of The Empire Strikes Back or&nbsp;
even the infamous Holiday Special, Boba Fett made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:23.760
his debut at an annual fall parade in San Anselmo,&nbsp;
California, on Sunday, September 24th, 1978.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.040
San Anselmo was home to 52 Park Way, often&nbsp;
known as George Lucas' "secret lair" — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.760
is why it was chosen as the site of the beloved&nbsp;
bounty hunter's premiere. Assistant film editor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.240
Duwayne Dunham wore the famous armor in the&nbsp;
parade simply because he was the right size.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.840
"Sure, have him turn around and get a shot&nbsp;
at the jetpack. Can you do it, Duwayne?"

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.560
According to Dunham, the parade took place on&nbsp;
an extremely hot day in San Anselmo, which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.360
recalled "could have been 100 degrees." Since&nbsp;
no one knew who his character was at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.680
few fans approached him, though Dunham&nbsp;
did, in fact, sign autographs. For this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.400
he even had to ask a nearby producer, Gary&nbsp;
Kurtz, if "Fett" had one T in it, or two.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.080
The first onscreen appearance of Boba Fett&nbsp;
came two months after the San Anselmo parade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.760
during the now-notorious Star Wars&nbsp;
Holiday Special. The bounty hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.920
shows up in an animated sequence, in&nbsp;
which he is voiced by Don Francks.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.160
"I take it you have no love of the Empire."

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.360
After the Millennium Falcon crashes on a water&nbsp;
planet, Luke and the droids try to rescue their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.240
friends. As soon as they land, however, they're&nbsp;
threatened by a large beast that Fett fights off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.000
thus gaining the heroes' trust. In&nbsp;
truth, he's been sent by the Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.600
to infiltrate the rebels in the hopes&nbsp;
of finding the location of their base.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.080
Francks was a Canadian actor who worked&nbsp;
regularly up until his passing in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.040
at the age of 84. His most visible roles&nbsp;
include Walter on the Canadian TV series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.560
La Femme Nikita and Chief Jake Newby in the&nbsp;
1981 slasher classic My Bloody Valentine.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.280
Most fans didn't get to meet Boba Fett until&nbsp;
he went on the hunt for Han Solo in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.840
Empire Strikes Back. For most of Boba Fett's&nbsp;
scenes in that movie and Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.880
the man in the suit is Jeremy&nbsp;
Bulloch. The English actor had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.680
appeared in multiple British TV series and&nbsp;
films before becoming part of Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.840
but his silent role as Boba Fett&nbsp;
remains by far his most well-remembered.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.520
Speaking to io9 in 2011, Bulloch said he never&nbsp;
expected Boba Fett's character to become so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.840
popular. When asked what he thought Boba got&nbsp;
up to after plunging into the sarlacc pit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.200
Bulloch suggested that Boba might have&nbsp;
tracked down and killed Mace Windu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.840
who killed Boba's father in Attack of the&nbsp;
Clones before appearing to die one movie later.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.360
Bulloch continued to act on-and-off&nbsp;
in the decades after Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.600
and even made a cameo appearance in&nbsp;
Revenge of the Sith. In December 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.600
he died at the age of 75 from&nbsp;
complications of Parkinson's disease.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Jeremy Bulloch's face only appears once&nbsp;
during The Empire Strikes Back — and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.800
isn't while he's playing Boba. When Luke&nbsp;
Skywalker infiltrates Bespin's Cloud City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.680
Leia spots him and warns that he's falling into&nbsp;
a trap. At the same time, an Imperial officer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.000
is trying to pull her away. That officer is&nbsp;
Lieutenant Sheckil, played by Bulloch. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.720
production's shooting schedules conflicted at the&nbsp;
time, however, and in order to shoot that scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.640
Bulloch couldn't play Boba Fett in another&nbsp;
— one in which the bounty hunter walks down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.080
a hallway with Vader, Lando, and Lobot. So John&nbsp;
Morton got to be Boba Fett for that one scene.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.880
That isn't Morton's only role in Empire,&nbsp;
either. He also played rebel pilot Dak Ralter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.280
Luke Skywalker's gunner during the Battle of&nbsp;
Hoth. Having previously worked as a stuntman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.440
Morton didn't do much more acting during his&nbsp;
career, although he did score smaller roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.040
in two other sci-fi classics released the same&nbsp;
year as Empire — Flash Gordon and Superman II.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.920
While Jeremy Bulloch and John Morton played the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.120
physical role of Boba Fett in&nbsp;
the original Star Wars trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.920
the voice fans initially heard coming out&nbsp;
of Fett's helmet was that of Jason Wingreen.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.200
"What if he doesn't survive?&nbsp;
He's worth a lot to me."

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.720
This isn't the case for many&nbsp;
modern Star Wars fans, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.000
as the 2004 DVD releases swapped&nbsp;
Wingreen's voice with Temuera Morrison's.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.880
"What if he doesn't survive?&nbsp;
He's worth a lot to me."

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.480
Wingreen claimed that, for him, portraying Boba&nbsp;
Fett took "no more than 10 minutes" of work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.600
There's an argument to be made that&nbsp;
Wingreen wasn't treated very fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.000
by the Star Wars franchise after lending&nbsp;
his voice to the famous bounty hunter. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.960
name wasn't included in the&nbsp;
credits of Empire Strikes Back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.920
and his involvement wasn't even public knowledge&nbsp;
until around the year 2000. Most shockingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.480
he never received any residual compensation&nbsp;
for his work, even though his voice was used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.480
for Boba Fett action figures. Wingreen died&nbsp;
on Christmas Day, 2015. He was 95 years old.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.880
Back in 1981, George Lucas sold the radio&nbsp;
rights for the original Star Wars trilogy to NPR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.560
for the whopping sum of $1; the filmmaker had been&nbsp;
listening to NPR since his college days, and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.920
inclined to feel generous towards them. All three&nbsp;
movies were eventually adapted into radio dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.720
split into half-hour-long installments. Certain&nbsp;
actors from the movies reprised their roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.720
but most of the other characters had&nbsp;
to be recast — including Boba Fett.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.840
In the 1983 Empire Strikes Back radio play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.440
Boba Fett was played by Alan Rosenberg, an actor&nbsp;
who has worked steadily since the late 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.160
mostly in television. Unfortunately, by the time&nbsp;
a Return of the Jedi adaptation could be made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.600
federal funding cuts and a number of other factors&nbsp;
had put the radio play on hold. It wasn't until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:23.840
1996 that the six-part drama was recorded, with&nbsp;
Ed Begley Jr. taking over the role of Boba Fett.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.680
Around the same time, in 1994, the popular Dark&nbsp;
Empire comic book series was adapted into two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.480
audio dramas. For the role of Boba Fett,&nbsp;
character actor Joseph Hacker was chosen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.600
Hacker has appeared on dozens of TV shows&nbsp;
over the years, from Quantum Leap to 24.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.720
The final actor to play Fett in an audio drama was&nbsp;
the otherwise-unknown Tim Glovatsky, who played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.600
the bounty hunter in Dark Forces: Rebel Agent,&nbsp;
an adaptation of the novel of the same name.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.000
When the Special Edition of A&nbsp;
New Hope hit theaters in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.080
it included an uncompleted scene from the&nbsp;
original production, in which Jabba the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.960
Hutt and Han Solo talk before the Falcon leaves&nbsp;
Tatooine. Along with a group of armed henchmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.720
Jabba is accompanied by the silent&nbsp;
but intimidating figure of Boba Fett.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.960
The bounty hunter was worked into the&nbsp;
story digitally, courtesy of Industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.280
Light and Magic animator Mark Austin, who&nbsp;
donned the iconic armor for the scene.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.600
While his appearance is brief in A New Hope,&nbsp;
Austin obviously feels attached to the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.080
He even played the bounty hunter for a second&nbsp;
time on the Boba Fett Fan Club's web series No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.400
Disintegrations. These episodes are meant&nbsp;
to be transmissions from Boba Fett's ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.600
most of which take place within&nbsp;
specific chapters of the saga.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.760
When it came time to produce the&nbsp;
Special Edition of Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.680
George Lucas apparently wanted more Boba&nbsp;
Fett — even though he never did manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.000
work in an escape from the sarlacc pit. What&nbsp;
fans do get, however, is more time with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.400
armored bounty hunter during the extended song&nbsp;
and dance number in Jabba the Hutt's palace.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.520
For most of these new Boba Fett&nbsp;
shots, special effects artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:56.480
Don Bies stepped into the character's iconic&nbsp;
armor. Nelson Hall, another VFX artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.280
provided the rest of the footage for Fett.&nbsp;
In a 2016 interview with StarWars.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.520
Bies revealed he also played a musician in&nbsp;
Jabba's band, as well as a whole bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.640
stormtroopers and Imperial officers in A New Hope.&nbsp;
Apparently, he and Nelson Hall stuck themselves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.800
so many scenes that their effects supervisor&nbsp;
actually had to ask them to tone it down.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.760
If you're a fan of animated Star Wars&nbsp;
properties, you've likely heard Tom Kane's voice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.600
He's the narrator of Star Wars: The Clone&nbsp;
Wars, as well as the voice of Yoda in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.000
series and a number of other productions.&nbsp;
As far as Boba Fett is concerned, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.880
Kane has portrayed the bounty hunter three&nbsp;
times — albeit only in Star Wars video games.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.120
Kane's first outing as Boba Fett came&nbsp;
during 2000's Star Wars: Demolition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.200
a game in which players pit different vehicles&nbsp;
from the franchise against each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.960
Kane voiced Fett once again in the 2001 real-time&nbsp;
strategy game Star Wars: Galactic Battlegrounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.440
Finally, he played Fett in 2003's&nbsp;
Star Wars Jedi Knight: Jedi Academy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.320
which has a particularly special&nbsp;
place in many fans' hearts.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.000
"Boba Fett!"

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.920
"This doesn't concern you, Jedi. Just walk away."

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.640
Attack of the Clones reveals a whole lot&nbsp;
about Boba Fett. First, it's shown that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.600
bounty hunter is a clone created as payment&nbsp;
to Jango Fett by the clone makers of Kamino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.360
in exchange for the use of his DNA to&nbsp;
create their clone army. The movie also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.680
makes clear that Boba Fett's appreciation&nbsp;
of violence emerged at a young age. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.080
it's revealed that Fett saw his father die on&nbsp;
Geonosis at Mace Windu's hands. The image of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.560
young Boba Fett holding Jango's helmet is one&nbsp;
of the most memorable of the prequel trilogy.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.280
Young Boba Fett is played by the New Zealand-born&nbsp;
actor Daniel Logan. Playing Boba left an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.840
interesting mark on the young performer, too&nbsp;
— when Looper spoke to him in April 2020, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:41.040
revealed that, like Jake Lloyd, he suffered plenty&nbsp;
of bullying for his role in the trilogy. He said:

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.440
"I think we're very, very blessed that we came&nbsp;
in during the era right before social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.520
and this whole entire internet explosion,&nbsp;
because I think it could've turned out worse."

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.320
Luckily, this hasn't stopped him&nbsp;
embracing his connection with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.400
Star Wars saga — and when Logan got the&nbsp;
call to reprise his role on The Clone Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.320
he pretty much jumped at the opportunity.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.080
In 1998, LucasArts and Nintendo teamed up&nbsp;
to release the flight action game Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.200
Rogue Squadron. In this game, the&nbsp;
player guides the eponymous rebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.920
group through a variety of mission objectives.&nbsp;
It proved popular enough to spawn two sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.000
including 2003's Star Wars:&nbsp;
Rogue Squadron III: Rebel Strike.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.120
Actor Chris Cox played multiple&nbsp;
characters in Rebel Strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.920
including Boba Fett. His Fett performance is&nbsp;
spotlighted by a mission that recreates Leia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.320
futile pursuit of the bounty hunter when&nbsp;
he leaves Bespin with the frozen Han Solo.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.760
While Rebel Strike is the only game&nbsp;
in which Cox portrayed Boba Fett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:35.840
it's only one of many Star Wars games he's&nbsp;
worked on. The prolific voice actor can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.480
heard across the Star Wars galaxy, from Fallen&nbsp;
Order to Battlefront to The Force Unleashed.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.480
While fans in his home country of New&nbsp;
Zealand were already familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:48.400
his work before he stepped into the Star&nbsp;
Wars universe, it was Temuera Morrison's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:52.480
pitch-perfect performance as Jango Fett in&nbsp;
Attack of the Clones that made him known to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.120
audiences around the world. Since that film&nbsp;
establishes that Boba is a clone of Jango,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.880
it also laid the foundation for Morrison&nbsp;
to soften the disappointment so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.680
fans suffered in Return of the Jedi by finally&nbsp;
returning Boba Fett to the land of the living.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.840
"Oh yeah, we got the Mandalorian&nbsp;
blues, on the sands of Tatooine."

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.720
Morrison voiced Boba Fett a number of times before&nbsp;
he played him in a live-action capacity, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.080
When the Special Editions of the original trilogy&nbsp;
got new home releases in 2004, Jason Wingreen's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.000
voice was replaced with Morrison's. Morrison&nbsp;
went on to voice the character in a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:35.120
of video games, including 2005's Battlefront II&nbsp;
and 2006's Empire at War. More recently, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.840
second season of The Mandalorian, Morrison's&nbsp;
Boba Fett joins up with Din Djarin to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:45.120
rescue Grogu from the clutches of Moff Gideon.&nbsp;
Try saying that six times…. That season ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:50.880
with the revelation that Fett will be getting&nbsp;
his own solo series on Disney+ in December 2021.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:56.640
Dee Bradley Baker has enjoyed a long, varied,&nbsp;
and incredibly successful voice acting career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.000
including a whole lot of work in&nbsp;
the Star Wars universe. He's perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.160
best known for voicing Captain Rex in The&nbsp;
Clone Wars, Rebels, and The Bad Batch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.280
though he also played a number of&nbsp;
clone troopers in each series..

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:10.800
Baker portrayed Boba Fett in both&nbsp;
of the Force Unleashed games, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.240
In 2011, the year after Force&nbsp;
Unleashed II was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.040
fans were able to hear him voice the bounty hunter&nbsp;
once again, albeit as part of a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.480
production. That year, Disney's Hollywood Studios&nbsp;
debuted Star Tours — The Adventures Continue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.360
an attraction that sees Baker reprise his role&nbsp;
as the galaxy's most dangerous bounty hunter.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:37.280
Check out one of our newest videos right here!&nbsp;
Plus, even more Looper videos about Boba Fett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:49.840
are coming soon. Subscribe to our YouTube channel&nbsp;
and hit the bell so you don't miss a single one.

